error":false,"num_turns":1,"subtype":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"{\"grade\":\"correct\",\"rationale\":\"The answer covers the central requested facts without a material error. It states that the fund is a separate legal entity that owns its assets. It says the assets are held by an independent custodian under the 1940 Act custody rules, and that sponsor creditors normally have no claim on them. It also says sponsor insolvency does not cancel the shares, since they are interests in the fund rather than claims on the sponsor. It frames separation as the structural default rather than a guarantee, as the question asks. It names the board's options (replacement, reorganization or liquidation) after contracts terminate. Its caveats (commingling or fraud, affiliated sweep or securities-lending exposures, operational disruption, licensing) are reasonable and appropriately hedged. One minor omission: it does not say that in a liquidation shareholders receive their pro rata share of net assets. It also does not explicitly note that investment losses remain possible or that this analysis does not apply to ETNs. Since it already says shares are not wiped out, these gaps are incidental and do not undermine the central conclusion.\"}","ttft_ms":3192,"type":"result","duration_ms":6162,"uuid":"3341e529-712e-40e0-9842-2fb5ec5c0cf2","ttft_stream_ms":572,"time_to_request_ms":74,"first_content_frame_ms":572,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0,"credential_expires_at":"2026-11-28T06:36:39.484Z"}
